# Tomcat's HTTP/2 HPACK decoder accepted any bytes a client sent in a header
# field name or value. HpackDecoder.readHpackString decoded the length-prefixed
# string literal straight off the connection's ByteBuffer and
# HPackHuffman.decode walked the Huffman tree appending each decoded
# character, and neither path asked whether the character is legal in an HTTP
# field: names are not checked against the token grammar or rejected for
# upper case, values are not checked against field-vchar/field-content, so
# control characters and forbidden bytes reached the header store that every
# downstream consumer (logging, proxying, the servlet API) trusts. The HTTP/1.1
# parser had enforced exactly this via HttpParser for years; the HTTP/2 path
# never validated at all, so over HTTP/2 the container accepted headers it
# would have rejected over HTTP/1.1 (CWE-20, CWE-113 adjacent).
#
# The fix 19f17a257 threads an isFieldName flag through both decode paths and
# validates every character before appending it: HttpParser.isToken plus a
# lower-case test for names, HttpParser.isFieldVChar / isFieldContent for
# values. It also moves the lower-case check out of Stream.emitHeader into the
# decoder, where the grammar check now lives.
#
# Detected by VYQL-VAL-038 (code.StructuredInputDecode), on a Java binding
# that asks for the decode shape rather than for any Tomcat name: a method
# holding a java.nio.ByteBuffer that reads bytes out of it and appends into a
# builder, with no character-grammar check anywhere in the method. No library
# documents a private decode helper, so there is no callee to bind and the
# shape is the API. The ByteBuffer restriction is what makes the shape
# shippable: the wider trigger the first pass measured -- any loop appending
# characters read from a source into a builder, Reader and iterator included
# -- selects 406 methods in Tomcat's own java tree alone, because string
# building and accumulator loops are ordinary code. Restricting the input to
# a ByteBuffer keeps it on binary wire decoding.
#
# Measured, --cache off, three runs per revision over the whole java/ subtree
# at 19f17a257^ and at 19f17a257: 1902 findings at both revisions before the
# binding, identical fingerprints; 1905 at the parent and 1903 at the fix with
# it, stable across all six runs. The delta is exactly the two decode methods
# -- HpackDecoder.readHpackString and HPackHuffman.decode -- which report at
# the parent and go to zero at the fix, plus one site the fix does not touch:
# WsWebSocketContainer.readLine, which reads an HTTP response header line off
# a ByteBuffer during the client handshake and splits on ':' with no character
# check at either revision. That is the same weakness in the same repository,
# not collateral against the pair.
#
# Collateral across the sibling Java clones, one bounded run each, reported as
# the delta over the same tree without the binding: undertow +14 (its own
# HpackDecoder.readHpackString and HPackHuffman.decode, HTTP/1.1 header value
# and reason-phrase and status-code and query-parameter readers, AJP request
# and response parsers, the chunked-trailer reader, the proxy-protocol
# reader, one test helper), quarkus resteasy-reactive +1 (the form-urlencoded
# body parser), and zero on woodstox, protobuf, batik, publiccms, cloudfoundry
# uaa, vert.x, zookeeper, xstream and spring-security -- about 8,600
# application Java files reporting nothing. protobuf and silverpeas-core were
# not measured to a count: protobuf aborts at the 6GB ceiling and
# silverpeas-core with it, and tika dies in the scanner's existing slice-bounds
# crash, all before this binding is reached. Two of the fourteen undertow rows
# are false positives worth naming: handlePath does validate, through the
# ALLOWED_TARGET_CHARACTER boolean-array lookup rather than a call, and
# MimeDecodingTestCase is a test. A table-driven check is invisible to a
# call-token negation; that is the known residual and it is the
# false-positive direction.
#
# The negation lists the character checks such a loop can consult -- the RFC's
# own rule names, the JDK's java.lang.Character classification they are
# usually written with, and the validator names the Java HTTP implementations
# publish -- and any of them in any position in the method discharges the
# obligation. The fourth case below pins the one that matters most: a guard
# sitting in the loop condition rather than around the append suppresses the
# finding, which is the spelling HTTP/1.1 uses, and which no Call node records
# (vyql query for isToken in HttpParser.java returns the two if-statement call
# sites and neither while condition) while the method context token does. The
# fifth case pins the confinement: the Reader-backed HTTP/1.1 token loop,
# textually the same append, is not reported because it does not hold a
# ByteBuffer.
#
# The residual on the other side is unchanged from the first pass: validation
# that lives in the caller or in the store the decoded text is handed to is
# invisible to a method-scoped negation, so a design that centralises the
# check downstream of the decoder is reported at the decoder and cleared
# nowhere. VYQL-INJ-247 stays rejected on every case below -- this code
# disables no flag, and that concept is a header store constructed with
# validation switched off, not a decoder that never had one.

test "rank1858: an HPACK field string decoded straight off the request bytes"
  lang java
  expect VYQL-VAL-038
  reject VYQL-INJ-247
  code
  ```
  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  class HpackDecoder {
      // Decodes a HPACK string literal -- an HTTP/2 header field name or
      // value -- from the request bytes.
      private String readHpackString(ByteBuffer buffer) {
          byte data = buffer.get(buffer.position());
          int length = Hpack.decodeInteger(buffer, 7);
          boolean huffman = (data & 0b10000000) != 0;
          if (huffman) {
              return readHuffmanString(length, buffer);
          }
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
              stringBuilder.append((char) buffer.get());
          }
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }

      private String readHuffmanString(int length, ByteBuffer buffer) {
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          HPackHuffman.decode(buffer, length, stringBuilder);
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1858: the Huffman walk appends each decoded character with no grammar check"
  lang java
  expect VYQL-VAL-038
  code
  ```
  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  class HPackHuffman {
      private static final int[] DECODING_TABLE = new int[256];
      private static final int LOW_MASK = 0xFFFF;
      private static final int LOW_TERMINAL_BIT = 1 << 16;
      private static final int HIGH_TERMINAL_BIT = 1 << 31;

      public static void decode(ByteBuffer data, int length, StringBuilder target) {
          int treePos = 0;
          for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
              byte b = data.get();
              int bitPos = 7;
              while (bitPos >= 0) {
                  int val = DECODING_TABLE[treePos];
                  if (((1 << bitPos) & b) == 0) {
                      if ((val & LOW_TERMINAL_BIT) == 0) {
                          treePos = val & LOW_MASK;
                      } else {
                          target.append((char) (val & LOW_MASK));
                          treePos = 0;
                      }
                  } else {
                      if ((val & HIGH_TERMINAL_BIT) == 0) {
                          treePos = (val >> 16) & LOW_MASK;
                      } else {
                          target.append((char) ((val >> 16) & LOW_MASK));
                          treePos = 0;
                      }
                  }
                  bitPos--;
              }
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1858: the fix validates every character before appending it"
  lang java
  reject VYQL-VAL-038
  reject VYQL-INJ-247
  code
  ```
  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  class HpackDecoder {
      private String readHpackString(ByteBuffer buffer, boolean isFieldName) {
          byte data = buffer.get(buffer.position());
          int length = Hpack.decodeInteger(buffer, 7);
          boolean huffman = (data & 0b10000000) != 0;
          if (huffman) {
              return readHuffmanString(length, buffer, isFieldName);
          }
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
              char c = (char) (buffer.get() & 0xFF);
              if (isFieldName) {
                  if (HttpParser.isToken(c) && !Character.isUpperCase(c)) {
                      stringBuilder.append(c);
                  } else {
                      throw new IllegalArgumentException("illegal character in name");
                  }
              } else {
                  if ((i == 0 || i == length - 1) && HttpParser.isFieldVChar(c) ||
                          i > 0 && i < length - 1 && HttpParser.isFieldContent(c)) {
                      stringBuilder.append(c);
                  } else {
                      throw new IllegalArgumentException("illegal character in value");
                  }
              }
          }
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }

      private String readHuffmanString(int length, ByteBuffer buffer, boolean isFieldName) {
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          HPackHuffman.decode(buffer, length, stringBuilder, isFieldName);
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1858: a guard in the loop condition is as good as one around the append"
  lang java
  reject VYQL-VAL-038
  code
  ```
  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  class FieldReader {
      // The HTTP/1.1 spelling of the same obligation, moved onto a buffer:
      // the grammar test gates the loop rather than the append.
      static String readToken(ByteBuffer buffer) {
          StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
          int c = buffer.get();
          while (c != -1 && HttpParser.isToken(c)) {
              result.append((char) c);
              c = buffer.get();
          }
          return result.toString();
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1858: the Reader-backed token loop is string building, not wire decoding"
  lang java
  reject VYQL-VAL-038
  code
  ```
  import java.io.IOException;
  import java.io.Reader;

  class HttpParser {
      // HttpParser.readToken as it ships: same append, same guard in the loop
      // condition, but the input is a Reader and not a ByteBuffer.
      static String readToken(Reader input) throws IOException {
          StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
          int c = input.read();
          while (c != -1 && isToken(c)) {
              result.append((char) c);
              c = input.read();
          }
          return result.toString();
      }

      static boolean isToken(int c) {
          return c > 0x20 && c < 0x7f;
      }
  }
  ```
